#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:900
msgid "William Thomson (Lord Kelvin) advanced absolute thermodynamic temperature. The kelvin starts at absolute zero and uses the same degree size as Celsius. Equations of gas behaviour and statistical mechanics prefer absolute scales because ratios and absolute values behave cleanly without arbitrary offsets."
msgstr "William Thomson (Lord Kelvin) avanzó la temperatura termodinámica absoluta. El kelvin comienza en el cero absoluto y utiliza el mismo tamaño de grados que Celsius. Las ecuaciones del comportamiento de los gases y la mecánica estadística prefieren escalas absolutas porque las proporciones y los valores absolutos se comportan limpiamente sin compensaciones arbitrarias."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1785
msgid "Wind reports in marine forecasts often use knots. A “20-knot wind” is about 37 km/h or 23 mph. Small-craft advice thresholds are written in knots in many countries; convert only after you understand the category boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2248
